# G126 — MP3 to Wav Converter

*v2. See `mp3-wav-converter.md` for prose: time-axis typed buffer,
integer-scaled linear resample, two-pass normalize-to-peak, RIFF LE
serialization, i16 as sole pipeline intermediate.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Audio Archive Export

[audio-export @input-path @output-path @target-rate
    IN ORDER TO
        decode an MP3 file into PCM
        resample to a target rate without floating-point drift
        normalize to peak and downmix to mono
        encode as WAV bytes and save to the vault

    @frames <- [mp3[parse-frames {bytes: [vault[load-bytes {path: @input-path}]]}]]
    @buf    <- [mpw[decode-mp3-to-buffer {frames: @frames}]]
    @buf    <- [mpw[resample {buffer: @buf, dst-rate: @target-rate}]]
    @buf    <- [mpw[normalize-to-peak {buffer: @buf, target: 32000}]]
    @buf    <- [mpw[to-mono {buffer: @buf}]]
    @wav    <- [mpw[encode-wav {buffer: @buf}]]

    [vault[save-bytes {path: @output-path, data: @wav}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[mpw
    decode-mp3-to-buffer    {frames}                ;; || no-frames, mixed-formats
    resample                {buffer, dst-rate}      ;; || sample-rate-zero
    normalize-to-peak       {buffer, target}
    to-mono                 {buffer}
    to-stereo               {buffer}
    encode-wav              {buffer}
    decode-wav              {bytes}                  ;; || empty-buffer
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: integer-scaled trig commitment as `where`

The v1 baked integer-scaled linear interpolation into the resample
code for cross-language determinism. In Innate, the contract is
part of the resolver agreement:

[mpw[resample {buffer: @b, dst-rate: @r}
    where
        source position = i × src-rate × 1000 / dst-rate
        and output sample = a + (b - a) × frac / 1000
            where frac = src-pos mod 1000
        and all arithmetic is integer (no floats across the boundary)
        and output is byte-identical across all conforming resolvers
]

This is not implementation detail — it's the cross-resolver contract.
A Rust resolver, a Common Lisp resolver, and a Python resolver must
all produce the same output bytes. The `where` clause documents the
obligation.

---

## Two-pass normalize as ordered commissions

[mpw[normalize-to-peak {buffer: @b, target: @target}
    where
        pass 1 scans for max-abs across all samples
        and pass 2 scales every sample by target / max-abs
        and both passes are deterministic over the same input
]

---

## RIFF byte layout as constraint

[mpw[encode-wav {buffer: @b}
    where
        bytes 0-3 are "RIFF"
        and bytes 4-7 are the 36 + data-size little-endian u32
        and bytes 8-11 are "WAVE"
        and "fmt " chunk has 16-byte body with PCM format code 1
        and "data" chunk size is samples × 2 (16-bit PCM)
        and all integers are little-endian
        and chunk sizes exclude the 8-byte chunk header
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @frames <- [
        {sample-rate: 22050, channels: 2, samples-per-frame: 4,
         pcm: [100, -100, 200, -200, 400, -400, 800, -800]}
        {sample-rate: 22050, channels: 2, samples-per-frame: 2,
         pcm: [-500, 500, -1000, 1000]}
    ]
    @buf        <- [mpw[decode-mp3-to-buffer {frames: @frames}]]
    @resampled  <- [mpw[resample           {buffer: @buf, dst-rate: 44100}]]
    @normalized <- [mpw[normalize-to-peak  {buffer: @resampled, target: 32000}]]
    @mono       <- [mpw[to-mono            {buffer: @normalized}]]
    @wav        <- [mpw[encode-wav         {buffer: @mono}]]

    -> @wav:length                    ;; -> 68
    -> @wav[0..4]                      ;; -> "RIFF"
    -> @wav[8..12]                     ;; -> "WAVE"

    @decoded <- [mpw[decode-wav {bytes: @wav}]]
    -> @decoded:samples == @mono:samples     ;; -> true (byte-identical roundtrip)
]
